+### Presets
+
+A preset is a name for such a line, one for each display. `preset save` writes down the arrangement you have, `preset load` brings it back:
+
+<!-- windows: Safari, Mail, Notes -->
+```console
+$ arborctl layout "Safari:2 | (Mail / Notes)"
+arranged 3 windows
+$ arborctl preset save Writing
+saved Writing: Safari:2 | (Mail / Notes) | Desktop:0.75
+$ arborctl float-all
+floated 3 windows
+```
+<!-- closes: 103 -->
+```console
+$ arborctl preset load Writing
+loaded Writing: arranged 2 windows; waiting for Notes
+$ arborctl presets
+Writing  Safari:2 | (Mail / Notes) | Desktop:0.75
+```
+
+Notes was closed meanwhile. Its place waits for three minutes: open Notes and it goes there (or load with `--open`, and Arbor opens it). Windows that are not mentioned float. Presets live in
+`presets.json`, beside `settings.json`: names and lines, made to be edited by hand. Put the ones you use on keys:
+
+<!-- settings -->
+```json
+"bindings": {
+  "ctrl+opt+cmd+1": "preset load Writing",
+  "ctrl+opt+cmd+2": "preset load Desk --open"
+}
+```
+
+`arborctl bind ctrl+opt+cmd+1 preset load Writing` does the same from a terminal. The menu's Presets submenu lists them, and saves (Save This Arrangement…) without a terminal. [docs/PRESETS.md](docs/PRESETS.md) has all of it.
