diff --git a/collab/QUESTIONS.md b/collab/QUESTIONS.md
index 9f335bef..2b2d4c4b 100644
--- a/collab/QUESTIONS.md
+++ b/collab/QUESTIONS.md
@@ -1021,6 +1021,34 @@ One line per product decision made while building, newest last.
 - F54: which tab shows is not written; it is the group's most recently focused window.
 - F54: `--open` opens at most 12 apps, in the background (`open -g`), waits 60 s, and comes before rules but not before `ignore`. The wait ends as soon as the display's tree is not the one `layout` built. A latecomer never moves the view. It is refused from URLs.
 - F54: plain names are letters, digits, `.`, `-`, `_`; all other punctuation needs quotes, which keeps every other sign free for later. `,` in particular stays free for F50's several commands in one call.
+- F21: a preset is a name for one line of F54's notation, or a list of lines that each begin with their display (`@1 …`). It keeps nothing a line cannot say except the display: not the view, saved views or where floating windows were. Names of spaces (F20) will travel when the notation can write them.
+- F21: `presets.json` is an object of name → line (or list of lines), with no wrapper and no version: an entry of a kind this Arbor does not know is skipped with a reason and left in the file, which is how a later format can be added.
+- F21: the file is read every time it is needed and never cached, so an edit counts at once and there is no `reload`.
+- F21: `preset save` keeps every display. With one display connected the line names no display and loads wherever the pointer is; with several, each line names its own, and a display with nothing docked is written `@2 Desktop`, so loading puts that back too. `preset save @2 <name>` keeps one display, without naming it.
+- F21: saving under a name that exists replaces it without asking and says so; the file keeps the first spelling and the entry's place.
+- F21: titles are written only for apps that have several slots in the preset (over all its displays), 60 characters at most as in `layout --titles`. They are in the file and in what `presets` prints, never in replies or the log.
+- F21: names are at most 40 characters (as names of spaces are), capitals and spacing do not count, `/` counts as a space, no word begins with `--` or `@`.
+- F21: a line for a display that is not connected is skipped and reported, and its windows count as not mentioned. A preset whose lines name their displays cannot be sent elsewhere with `@n`.
+- F21: slots that find no window **always** wait after `preset load`, with or without `--open`; after `layout` only with `--open`, as F54 decided. Open: should plain `layout` wait too?
+- F21: the wait is three minutes (the number saved slots have after a launch) for `layout --open` as well, which waited one. One mechanism, one number.
+- F21: the wait no longer ends when the user drags a gap, docks or floats something: the place is in the tree and moves with it. It ends on a display with `arrange`, `float-all`, `layout` or `preset load` there, and everywhere with `pause`, quitting and the three minutes. This changes F54 ("the wait ends as soon as the display's tree is not the one `layout` built").
+- F21: for those three minutes every window of the line holds its place: one that closes (a splash screen, an app being started again) leaves the slot waiting. A window the user floated first leaves nothing.
+- F21: only a window that has just appeared takes a waiting slot. One that floats already (left out by the line, or floated by the user since) is never pulled in, whatever its title becomes.
+- F21: at most 50 slots wait at a time; a line can hold a thousand names.
+- F21: while a slot waits, moving its neighbour with ⌃⌥⌘+arrow can take a press that shows nothing, because the invisible place counts as a place, as that of a minimised window does. Accepted for three minutes.
+- F21: the menu loads a preset without opening apps; holding ⌥ loads it and opens them. Open: the other way round?
+- F21: a preset loaded by itself when displays come and go was built keyed by the number of displays and taken out again: a meeting room's display is "2" as well. Decided: it is to be keyed by what the display is (F25's `match` words) and to ask F58's `mayShowUnasked` (IDEAS I18). Until then no preset is ever loaded unasked.
+- F21: `preset load` is accepted from URLs without `--open`; `preset save`, `preset delete` and `presets` are not. `presets` works whatever Arbor is doing (paused, no permission); the others need it running.
+- F21: names of presets are kept out of the log, as F20 keeps names of spaces out (`‹name›`), whatever their capitals; so is any other word of a logged reply that reads like one of the file's names. A URL that is refused is logged as the command it is, without the name.
+- F21: a preset that is replaced or deleted is printed as the file had it, in the reply's text (not logged). There is still no `undo` for the file.
+- F21 and F54: `layout` and `preset load` count the saved views whose spaces are gone in the reply ("2 saved views are gone").
+- F21: `preset save` without a name opens F20's field; the field shows on the display commands act on, whichever display `@n` names. With nothing docked it does not open. A presets.json that cannot be read is said under the field before anything is typed, and ↩ does nothing.
+- F21 and F54: names of spaces are carried through `layout` and `preset load` as saved views are (`reset(tree:carrying:)`), and those that are lost are counted, in one place for every command that starts an arrangement afresh (`notingWhatIsLost`): "1 name is gone".
+- F21: ⌃⌥P stays reserved and unbound.
+- F21: when an arrangement is replaced (`layout`, `preset load`) and two named spaces have come to hold the same windows (a group down to one window, and that window's own place), the new tree has one space for both. Decided: the first name in list order keeps it and the other is counted as gone, because no space has two names (F20). Open: the name of the window's own place might be the better one to keep.
+- F21: when the three minutes are up a place goes as a saved slot does, because nothing moves by a timer: at once if no window has to move for it, otherwise with the next layout that somebody causes (`expireSlots`).
+- F21 and F23: while a place of a line waits on a display, other new windows float there though "Dock New Windows" is ticked, as F23 decided for `layout --open`: apps that start bring more than was asked for, and the arrangement stays as it was written until everybody has come. For a preset whose app is never opened that lasts the three minutes.
+- F21: when a late window takes its place and others float by then, a Desktop of the usual size (`arrangeDesktopWeight`) that would be too narrow to use is made just wide enough, as at the moment of loading. A Desktop written at any other size stays as written. F54's own wait did this by building the tree again.
 - (F23, 2026-10-14) One mechanism for new windows: `"newWindows": "dock"` is short for a last rule `{"app": "*", "action": "dock auto"}`. So rules win by coming first, late titles, F57 and F58 treat it like any rule, and `rules` shows it. Default `"float"`; which default TrellisWM's behaviour calls for is still Q13.
 - (F23, 2026-10-14) `*` at the end of a rule's `app` stands for the rest of the bundle id (`com.jetbrains.*`); `*` alone is any app, also a window without a bundle id.
 - (F23, 2026-10-14) The guards against small (under 400 x 300 as opened) and fixed-size windows belong to `dock auto` and to every `*` rule that docks, not to rules that name an app in full: whoever writes `com.apple.calculator` and `dock right` means it. A window that is passed over goes on to the next rule.
