+# Presets: arrangements with a name
+
+A day has a few arrangements: the laptop on its own, the laptop beside a big display, a call. A **preset** keeps one under a name, and brings it back with
+whichever windows of those apps are open then.
+
+<!-- windows: Safari, Mail, Notes, Music -->
+```console
+$ arborctl layout "Safari:2 | (Mail / Notes)"
+arranged 3 windows
+$ arborctl preset save Writing
+saved Writing: Safari:2 | (Mail / Notes) | Desktop:0.75
+$ arborctl float-all
+floated 3 windows
+$ arborctl preset load Writing
+loaded Writing: arranged 3 windows
+$ arborctl ascii
+display main
++------------------------------------++-----------------+...............
+|w101 Safari                         ||w102 Mail        |.desktop      .
+|                                    ||                 |.             .
+|                                    ||                 |.             .
+|                                    ||                 |~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+|                                    ||                 |~w104 Music   ~
+|                                    ||                 |~             ~
+|                                    ||                 |~             ~
+|                                    ||                 |~             ~
+|                                    ||                 |~             ~
+|                                    |+-----------------+~             ~
+|                                    |+-----------------+~             ~
+|                                    ||w103 Notes       |~             ~
+|                                    ||                 |~             ~
+|                                    ||                 |~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+|                                    ||                 |.             .
+|                                    ||                 |.             .
+|                                    ||                 |.             .
+|                                    ||                 |.             .
+|                                    ||                 |.             .
+|                                    ||                 |.             .
++------------------------------------++-----------------+...............
+camera n1
+```
+
+Arrange the windows any way you like (drag them, use the shortcuts, type a line); `preset save` writes down what is there. Music was floating, so it is not part of the
+preset, and loading leaves it floating.
+
+Every example on this page is run by the tests (`DocTranscriptTests`), against the real controller, and prints what it says here.
+
+## The commands
+
+| Command | What it does |
+|---|---|
+| `preset save <name>` | keeps the arrangement of every display under the name; a preset of that name is replaced |
+| `preset save @2 <name>` | keeps only that display's arrangement, for whichever display you load it on |
+| `preset save` | asks for the name in a field on screen, as Save This Arrangement… in the menu does |
+| `preset load <name>` | brings it back |
+| `preset load <name> --open` | and opens the apps that have no window |
+| `preset load @2 <name>` | on that display instead of the one you are working on |
+| `preset delete <name>` | forgets it |
+| `presets` | lists them |
+
+A name can have several words and needs no quotes: `preset save Deep Work`. Capitals are kept, and do not count when you ask for it: `preset load deep work`. It is at most 40 characters long, and
+no word of it begins with `--` or `@`.
+
+## The file
+
+Presets live in `presets.json`, beside `settings.json` in `~/Library/Application Support/Arbor`. It holds names and lines, in the notation of [`layout`](LAYOUTS.md), and is meant to be read, edited, kept in
+dotfiles and passed on:
+
+```console
+$ cat "$HOME/Library/Application Support/Arbor/presets.json"
+{
+  "Writing": "Safari:2 | (Mail / Notes) | Desktop:0.75"
+}
+```
+
+Write one by hand if you prefer. There is nothing to reload: the file is read whenever a preset is asked for.
+
+<!-- windows: Safari, Mail, Notes, Terminal, zoom.us -->
+<!-- presets.json -->
+```json
+{
+  "Writing": "Safari:2 | (Mail / Notes)",
+  "Call": "zoom.us:2 | Notes",
+  "Code": "[Safari Notes] | Terminal | Desktop:0.25"
+}
+```
+```console
+$ arborctl presets
+Writing  Safari:2 | (Mail / Notes)
+Call     zoom.us:2 | Notes
+Code     [Safari Notes] | Terminal | Desktop:0.25
+$ arborctl preset load call
+loaded Call: arranged 2 windows
+$ arborctl layout
+zoom.us:2 | Notes | Desktop:0.75
+```
+
+When Arbor changes the file it changes the one entry it was asked to change, and leaves every other byte as it was: the order, the spacing, and anything it does not understand.
+
+```console
+$ arborctl preset delete Code
+deleted Code
+it was: "[Safari Notes] | Terminal | Desktop:0.25"
+$ arborctl preset save Call
+saved Call, replacing what it was: zoom.us:2 | Notes | Desktop:0.75
+it was: "zoom.us:2 | Notes"
+$ cat "$HOME/Library/Application Support/Arbor/presets.json"
+{
+  "Writing": "Safari:2 | (Mail / Notes)",
+  "Call": "zoom.us:2 | Notes | Desktop:0.75"
+}
+```
+
+A preset that is replaced or deleted is printed as it was, so a `save` typed for a `load` can be put right by hand.
+
+**What a preset keeps** is what a line can say, and which display it is for: who sits where, the sizes, the tab groups, the Desktop. **What it does not keep**: where you were looking (loading shows
+everything), saved views, and where floating windows were. Views you saved and names you gave to spaces on the display stay if their spaces still hold the same windows after loading; if some do not, the reply says how many are gone.
+
+## Which window goes where
+
+A preset names apps, not windows: window numbers change each time an app starts. The rules are those of `layout` ([Names](LAYOUTS.md#names), [Several windows of one app](LAYOUTS.md#several-windows-of-one-app)): a slot takes the window of its app that was
+used last, and the next slot for the same app the one before.
+
+When a preset has several slots for one app, `preset save` also writes the beginning of each window's title, so that each finds its own place again. A title is a preference: if no window has it any more, the slot
+takes another window of the app.
+
+<!-- windows: Safari "Inbox", Safari "Docs", Mail "All mail" -->
+```console
+$ arborctl layout "101 | 103 | 102"
+arranged 3 windows
+$ arborctl preset save Two Browsers
+saved Two Browsers: Safari~… | Mail | Safari~… | Desktop:0.5
+$ arborctl presets
+Two Browsers  Safari~Inbox | Mail | Safari~Docs | Desktop:0.5
+```
+
+Titles are written only there, in your own file. Replies show `~…` in their place, because replies are logged and titles never are. Delete them from the file if you would rather not have them. The names you give your presets stay out of the
+log as well.
+
+**Windows that are not mentioned float**, as with `layout`.
+
+## Apps that are not open
+
+A slot whose app has no window is closed up, so its neighbours share the room. **It also waits**: if a window of that app appears within three minutes, it takes its place.
+
+<!-- windows: Safari, Terminal -->
+<!-- presets.json -->
+```json
+{
+  "Work": "Safari | (Slack / Terminal) | Desktop:0.5"
+}
+```
+```console
+$ arborctl preset load Work
+loaded Work: arranged 2 windows; waiting for Slack
+$ arborctl layout
+Safari | Terminal | Desktop:0.5
+```
+<!-- opens: Slack -->
+```console
+$ arborctl layout
+Safari | (Slack / Terminal) | Desktop:0.5
+```
+
+- The place is kept while you look around, drag a gap or dock something else: it moves with the arrangement, like the place of a minimised window.
+- It comes before any [rule](../README.md#rules-for-new-windows) for the app, except `ignore`. A window that no slot is waiting for is an ordinary new window, and rules apply.
+- A window of the preset that closes within the three minutes leaves its place waiting too, so a splash screen does not use up a slot, and an app you quit and start again returns to where it was.
+- The wait ends when the display is arranged anew (`arrange`, `float-all`, `layout`, another preset), when Arbor is paused or quits, and after three minutes.
+
+With `--open`, Arbor opens the apps that have no window at all, in the background, at most twelve at a time:
+
+<!-- windows: Safari -->
+<!-- presets.json -->
+```json
+{
+  "Work": "Safari | (Slack / Terminal) | Desktop:0.5"
+}
+```
+```console
+$ arborctl preset load Work --open
+loaded Work: arranged 1 window; opening Slack and Terminal
+```
+
+If none of a preset's apps has a window and nothing is being opened, nothing changes:
+
+<!-- windows: Music -->
+<!-- presets.json -->
+```json
+{
+  "Work": "Safari | (Slack / Terminal) | Desktop:0.5"
+}
+```
+```console
+$ arborctl preset load Work
+Work: no window of Safari, Slack or Terminal; nothing changed
+```
+
+## Several displays
+
+With more than one display connected, `preset save` writes a line for each, beginning with the display's number: `@1` is the main display, the others follow from left to right (as in `layout @2 …`).
+
+<!-- displays: 2 -->
+<!-- windows: Xcode, Terminal, Slack, Mail -->
+```console
+$ arborctl layout @1 "Xcode:2 | Terminal"
+arranged 2 windows
+$ arborctl layout @2 "Slack / Mail"
+arranged 2 windows
+$ arborctl preset save Desk
+saved Desk: @1 Xcode:2 | Terminal | Desktop:0.75; @2 (Slack / Mail) | Desktop:0.5
+$ cat "$HOME/Library/Application Support/Arbor/presets.json"
+{
+  "Desk": [
+    "@1 Xcode:2 | Terminal | Desktop:0.75",
+    "@2 (Slack / Mail) | Desktop:0.5"
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+Loading arranges all of them at once. Each window goes to one place only, whichever display it was on.
+
+```console
+$ arborctl float-all
+floated 4 windows
+$ arborctl preset load Desk
+loaded Desk: arranged 4 windows
+```
+
+A display with nothing docked is written as `@2 Desktop`, and loading puts that back too: what was docked there floats.
+
+A line for a display that is not connected is skipped, and its windows float like any that are not mentioned:
+
+<!-- connects: 1 -->
+```console
+$ arborctl preset load Desk
+loaded Desk: arranged 2 windows; display 2 is not connected
+```
+
+A preset of one line without a display goes to the display other commands act on (from a shortcut pressed while typing, the one with the focused window; otherwise the one under the pointer), or where you say. Sizes are relative, so one saved on a big display fits a small one.
+
+<!-- connects: 2 -->
+```console
+$ arborctl preset save @2 Chat
+saved Chat: (Slack / Mail) | Desktop:0.5
+$ arborctl preset load @1 Chat
+loaded Chat: arranged 2 windows, floated 2
+$ arborctl preset load @1 Desk
+Desk says itself which display each line is for; load it without @1
+```
+
+## Shortcuts, the menu, URLs
+
+A preset on a key is the quickest way to it. In `settings.json`:
+
+<!-- settings -->
+```json
+"bindings": {
+  "ctrl+opt+cmd+1": "preset load Solo",
+  "ctrl+opt+cmd+2": "preset load Desk --open",
+  "ctrl+opt+cmd+3": "preset load Call"
+}
+```
+
+or from a terminal, which writes the same into the file:
+
+<!-- windows: Safari -->
+```console
+$ arborctl bind ctrl+opt+cmd+1 preset load Solo
+ctrl+opt+cmd+1 runs `preset load Solo`
+```
+
+The menu bar item has a **Presets** submenu with one row for each preset; hold ⌥ to load one and open its apps. **Save This Arrangement…**, below them, opens a field for the name: the line under it says what ↩ will do ("↩ replaces the preset Desk"), ⎋ puts it away. `arborctl preset save`
+without a name opens the same field, so saving can be put on a key as well. **Copy Arrangement as Text** puts the line of the display you are working on on the pasteboard, to paste into the file, a shortcut or a chat. **Presets File…**, under
+Help, opens the file in your editor. **Go to…** (⌃⌥G) lists the presets after the windows, spaces and saved views: type a few letters of a name and press ↩.
+
+`arbor://preset/load/Desk` loads a preset from a link or another app; the words of a name are separated by slashes (`arbor://preset/load/Deep/Work`). URLs cannot save or delete presets, or open apps, unless `allowAllURLCommands` is on.
+
+<!-- windows: Safari, Mail -->
+<!-- presets.json -->
+```json
+{ "Deep Work": "Mail | Safari" }
+```
+```console
+$ open "arbor://preset/load/Deep/Work"
+$ arborctl layout
+Mail | Safari | Desktop:0.5
+```
+
+## Mistakes in the file
+
+An entry Arbor cannot read is skipped, and the others work. `presets` says which and why:
+
+<!-- windows: Safari, Mail, Notes -->
+<!-- presets.json with mistakes -->
+```json
+{
+  "Writing": "Safari | Mail / Notes",
+  "Reading": "Safari | Notes",
+  "Desk": ["@1 Safari", "Mail"],
+  "Later": 12
+}
+```
+```console
+$ arborctl presets
+Reading  Safari | Notes
+skipped "Writing": column 15: `|` and `/` are mixed here: put brackets around one of them
+skipped "Desk": with several lines, each begins with its display: @1, @2 and so on
+skipped "Later": expected a line in quotes, or a list of lines in [ and ]
+$ arborctl preset load Writing
+Writing in presets.json cannot be read: column 15: `|` and `/` are mixed here: put brackets around one of them
+$ arborctl preset load Reading
+loaded Reading: arranged 2 windows
+```
+
+Skipped entries stay in the file, whatever Arbor saves around them. If the file as a whole is not JSON, Arbor says where the mistake is and **does not write to it**, so a typo never costs you the rest:
+
+<!-- presets.json with mistakes -->
+```json
+{
+  "Writing": "Safari | (Mail / Notes)"
+  "Reading": "Safari | Notes"
+}
+```
+```console
+$ arborctl preset save Today
+presets.json cannot be read, and is left as it is: line 3, column 3: expected `,` or `}` after the value of `Writing`, found `"`
+$ arborctl presets
+presets.json cannot be read, and is left as it is: line 3, column 3: expected `,` or `}` after the value of `Writing`, found `"`
+```
