index 00000000..3fd87042
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/features/F21-presets.md
@@ -0,0 +1,160 @@
+# F21: Layout presets
+
+Written by an executor, 2026-10-14; for the documenter to review. From IDEAS I2.
+
+Status: done, headless; no part of it has run on a Mac yet: see "Not shipped"
+Depends on: F54 (the notation, the matcher, `--open`), `JSONText`, the slots that wait after a launch (`Controller+PendingSlots.swift`); works with F08, F11, F19, F20, F23, F27, F48
+Size: medium, nearly all of it headless
+
+## Summary
+
+A day has a few arrangements: the laptop alone, the laptop beside a big display, a call. Each takes a minute to build by hand and is gone when the next one is
+built. A **preset** is a name for an arrangement: `preset save Desk` keeps what is on the displays now, `preset load Desk` brings it back, with whichever windows
+of those apps exist then. TrellisWM has nothing like it.
+
+F54 gave arrangements a notation (`Safari | (Mail / Notes)`). A preset is a name for such a line, so there is nothing new to learn, and the file that holds them can be
+read, edited, kept in dotfiles and passed on.
+
+## Behaviour
+
+```
+arborctl preset save Desk            # keep the arrangement of every display under a name
+arborctl preset load Desk            # bring it back
+arborctl preset load Desk --open     # and open the apps that are not running
+arborctl preset delete Desk
+arborctl presets                     # what there is
+```
+
+### The file
+
+`presets.json` in Arbor's home folder, beside `settings.json`. A name, and the line:
+
+```json
+{
+  "Writing": "Safari | (Notes / Terminal)",
+  "Call": "zoom.us:2 | Notes",
+  "Desk": [
+    "@1 Xcode:2 | (Simulator / Terminal)",
+    "@2 Slack | Mail"
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+- A value is one line, or a list of lines that each begin with the display they are for (`@1` is the main display, as in `layout @1 …`). What follows is F54's notation, all of it.
+- **What a line cannot say, a preset does not keep**, except the display. Not the view (loading shows everything), not saved views, not where floating windows were. Names of
+  spaces (F20) travel if and when the notation can write them.
+- The file is read each time it is needed, so an edit counts at once; there is nothing to reload.
+- **An entry Arbor cannot read is skipped, with the reason** (`presets` lists them, the log has them), as rules are: a line with a mistake (the column is named), a value that is neither text nor a list of
+  text, a name used twice, two lines for one display, several lines of which one names no display. The other presets work.
+- **Arbor changes only the entry it was asked to change** and leaves every other byte as it was: order, spacing, entries it skipped, keys it does not know (`JSONText`, as for `settings.json`).
+- **A file that is not JSON is never written to.** `preset save` and `preset delete` fail and say where the typo is. So do `preset load` and `presets`. The same holds for a file that is read-only, is not
+  UTF-8, is a dangling link, is larger than 1 MB or has more than 1000 entries. A link is followed (dotfile managers), and the file keeps its permissions.
+
+### Names
+
+- A name is what follows the verb, words joined by one space: `preset save Deep Work`. Capitals are kept and do not count when looking a name up, nor does the kind or amount of space between words.
+- At most 40 characters (as names of spaces, F20), nothing invisible (as for names in the notation). A word cannot begin with `--` or `@`, which are options and displays.
+- **A name is never logged**, as names of spaces are not (F20): it may be a client or a project, and the log is what people paste into a bug report. The log has `preset load ‹name›: loaded ‹name›: arranged 3 windows`, and `entry 3` for an entry that was skipped. Of a file that cannot be read the reply quotes the text at the mistake, for whoever wrote it; the log and a trace get the place alone (`line 1, column 40`), as for settings.json.
+- In a URL the words are separated by slashes: `arbor://preset/load/Deep/Work`. So a slash counts as a space, in the file too.
+
+### Saving
+
+- `preset save <name…>` writes the arrangement of **every display**: with one display connected, one line without a display, which later loads on the display commands act on (F56: the one being typed on, or the one under the pointer); with several, one line for
+  each, `@1 …`, `@2 …`, including `@2 Desktop` for a display with nothing docked (loading puts that back too).
+- `preset save @2 <name…>` writes only that display's arrangement, without a display: a preset for "wherever I am".
+- **`preset save` without a name asks for one** in the field F20 built for naming spaces (`NamePromptModel.Subject.preset`): title "Save this arrangement as"; the line under the field says what ↩ would do ("↩ saves the preset Desk", "↩ replaces the preset Desk"), what is wrong with the name, or, before anything is typed, that presets.json cannot be read. ↩ runs `preset save <name>`; nothing typed, ⎋, a click elsewhere, a move of the view and `pause` put it away. It is what Save This Arrangement… in the menu runs.
+- Windows are written by app. **A title is written only where it is needed to tell windows apart**: when the preset has several slots for one app (`Safari~Inbox | Safari~Docs`). Titles are a preference, never a
+  condition (F54), and they are never logged or repeated in replies.
+- A window whose app has no name that can be written is left out, and the reply says so. A slot that is still waiting for its window (below) is written as it was.
+- Floating windows are not part of an arrangement and are not written.
+- With nothing docked anywhere there is nothing to save, and the command says so.
+- Saving under a name that exists replaces that preset and says so. The file keeps the name as it was first spelt, and its place.
+- **A preset that is replaced or deleted is printed as the file had it** (`it was: "Safari | Mail"`), in the reply's text, which is shown and not logged: a `save` typed for a `load` can be put right by hand.
+
+### Loading
+
+- All the slots of all the lines are matched against the windows in **one** pass of F54's matcher (by number, then title, then focus order, then `*`), so a window goes to one place only and a title asked for on one display is not
+  taken by a plain slot on another.
+- Each line is then applied as `layout` applies it: windows come over from other displays, **windows that are not mentioned float**, the view shows everything, floating windows move onto the Desktop.
+- Sizes are relative, so a preset saved on a display of another size or shape just works.
+- A line for a display that is not connected is skipped and the reply says so (`@2 Desktop` is skipped in silence). Its windows are treated as not mentioned.
+- `preset load @2 <name…>` sends a preset of one line to that display instead. It is refused for a preset whose lines name their displays.
+- If no window of any app in the preset exists (and nothing is being opened), nothing changes and the command fails, as `layout` does.
+- Loading the arrangement that is already there only shows everything. Saved views and history stay with the spaces that still hold the same windows (F54's `counterparts`); so do names of spaces (F20); the reply counts those that do not: `2 saved views are gone`, `1 name is gone`.
+- Floating windows stay in view, the one with the keyboard above all: a Desktop the line does not mention is made wide enough to use. A pinned window (F49) keeps its place and size.
+- **It is one command, so it is one step for `undo`** (F19), over all the displays it touched, from Go to… as well. The step is called `preset load` and Undo Load Preset, never by the name. Taking it back ends the wait; a window that came late adds no step.
+- It is refused while a window is being dragged or resized with the mouse.
+
+### Slots that wait
+
+- A slot that found no window is closed up (its neighbours share the room) **and waits**: when a window of that app appears within three minutes, it takes its slot, in the right place, ahead of any rule except
+  `ignore`. The reply says what is waited for: `loaded Desk: arranged 3 windows, floated 1; waiting for Slack`.
+- With `--open`, apps with no window at all are opened (at most 12, in the background, F54's launcher); the wait is the same.
+- The wait goes on while the user looks around, drags a gap or docks something else: the slot is a place in the tree, like that of a minimised window, and moves with it.
+- The wait ends: for a slot, when it is filled; for a display, with `arrange`, `float-all`, `layout` or another `preset load` there; for all, with `pause`, quitting, or three minutes (counted by the scheduler).
+- **Nothing moves when the time is up.** A place takes no room, so as a rule it just goes. Where its going would move a window all the same, it stays, overdue, until the next layout that somebody causes (`expireSlots`).
+- For those three minutes every window of the preset holds its place: one that closes (a splash screen, an app being started again) leaves its slot waiting. A window the user floated first leaves nothing.
+- Among several waiting slots a new window takes the one that asks for its title, otherwise the first as written.
+- Only a window that has just appeared takes a slot. One that floats already (left out by the preset, or floated by the user since) is never pulled in, whatever its title becomes.
+- At most 50 slots wait at a time.
+- A window that matches no waiting slot is an ordinary new window: rules (F11) apply.
+- It is the same mechanism that keeps saved slots for apps that restore their windows after login (`Controller+PendingSlots.swift`), and `layout --open` uses it too: one way of waiting.
+
+### When displays come and go
+
+Not built: **no preset is ever loaded unasked.** It was once built as `"presetsForDisplays": { "1": "Solo", "2": "Desk" }`, keyed by the number of connected displays, and that shape was decided against: a meeting room's display is "2" as well, and Desk would put Slack and Mail on it
+in front of the room. It is to be keyed by what the display **is**, in F25's `match` words (`builtin`, a name, `>=2500`), and to ask F58's `mayShowUnasked`, so that nothing rearranges itself during a call, a hold or mirroring. What was built and holds for the next shape too: two seconds
+after the last change, nothing for a display that only blinked, not at launch, not while paused, never with `--open`, an ordinary `preset load` (one step for undo).
+
+### Reach
+
+- **Menu** (`docs/MENU.md`, row 22): Presets ▸ has one row per preset (file order, at most 20; with ⌥ held, "Desk, Opening Its Apps"), then Save This Arrangement… (asks for the name in a one-field panel) and Copy Arrangement as Text. Rows that cannot act are grey and keep their place. Presets File… is under Help ▸, after Reload Settings.
+  A headless model (`PresetMenu`) says what the submenu holds; the app only draws it.
+- **Go to…** (⌃⌥G): presets are listed after windows, named spaces and saved views, by name, with what they arrange (no titles) and the note "preset". Choosing one loads it. `find <words>` goes to places only and never loads a preset.
+- **Shortcuts**: `"bindings": { "ctrl+opt+1": "preset load Desk" }`. ⌃⌥P stays reserved and unbound (`docs/KEYBOARD.md`).
+- **URLs**: `preset load` is an arrangement command and is allowed, without `--open`. `preset save` and `preset delete` change saved data, and `presets` is a query: refused unless `allowAllURLCommands` is on.
+
+## Design notes
+
+- `ArborCore/Preset.swift`: `Preset` (a name and its lines), `PresetFile` (text in, presets and reasons out; `saving` and `deleting` give the new text). Pure, on `LayoutNotation` and `JSONText`.
+- `ArborKit/Platform/PresetStore.swift`: the seam, which only moves text: `read() throws -> String?`, `write(_:) throws`. `FilePresetStore` shares `SettingsFile`'s reading and writing (links, permissions, read-only); `MemoryPresetStore` in ArborTesting.
+- `Controller+Presets.swift`: the five commands. No matcher, notation or launcher of its own: it joins the lines into one `Spec` to resolve them, and hands each line and its share of the windows to the code behind `layout`.
+- Waiting: an empty slot is filled with a stand-in window number before `Spec.tree(filling:)` builds the tree. `writtenSlots` maps the stand-in to the `WindowSlot` as written, and `WindowSlot.accepts` and `matchesTitle` decide who may take it.
+  Stand-ins of written slots have no hint, so the next launch drops them.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. `preset save` then `preset load`, after the windows were rearranged, floated, or closed and opened again, gives the same tree shape, sizes, tab groups and frames. On one display and on two.
+2. `presets.json` holds name → line as above; a hand-written file loads; saving and deleting change one entry and no other byte.
+3. Entries that cannot be read are skipped with a reason and survive a save; a file that is not JSON is never written to, and every command says where the mistake is.
+4. Names: capitals and spacing do not count, limits are enforced, text form and URL form round-trip.
+5. Titles are written only for apps with several slots; they never reach replies or the log. Names of presets never reach the log.
+6. One matcher pass over all lines: no window is placed twice; a title on display 2 wins over a plain slot on display 1.
+7. Windows not mentioned float; lines for missing displays are skipped and reported; a preset saved on another display size fills the display.
+8. A slot without a window waits and is taken in the right place: after view moves, a dragged gap, another dock; by title; before a rule but not before `ignore`; a splash screen leaves it waiting.
+9. The wait ends after three minutes of scheduler time, with `arrange`, `float-all`, `layout`, another load, `pause` and quitting; afterwards no stand-in is left in any tree, in `state`, `layout`, `status` or `state.json`.
+10. `--open` opens what is missing, through `WindowSystem.launchApp`; without it nothing is opened.
+11. Loading is one undo step on every display it touched (with F19).
+12. URL policy: `preset load` allowed, with `--open` refused; save, delete and `presets` refused.
+13. (Withdrawn: a preset loaded when displays come and go waits for F25 and F58.)
+14. The menu model lists the presets, Save This Arrangement… and Copy Arrangement as Text; the picker lists the presets.
+15. Random presets files, names and commands never crash Arbor, lose a window, dock one twice or break a tree.
+16. Every example in `docs/PRESETS.md` and the README is run by a test. `make check`, `make scenarios` and `make parity` pass.
+
+## Shipped / Not shipped
+
+Shipped: everything under Behaviour except what is listed below; criteria 1 to 12 and 14 to 16. The guide is `docs/PRESETS.md`; every example in it and in the README is run by a test.
+
+- `ArborCore`: `Preset.swift` (`Preset`, `Preset.Line`, `PresetFile`, `Spec.compactMapSlots`), `Command+Preset.swift`, `PresetMenu.swift`; five `Command` cases.
+- `ArborKit`: `Platform/PresetStore.swift` (`PresetStore`, `FilePresetStore`), `Controller+Presets.swift`, `Controller+WrittenSlots.swift`. `ArborTesting`: `MemoryPresetStore`, `Harness.presets`.
+- **One way of waiting.** `layout --open` kept the `Spec` and built the tree again when a window came (`pendingLayouts`). That is gone: its slots, and a preset's, wait as saved slots do after a launch, as stand-in numbers in the tree
+  (`Controller+PendingSlots.swift`, which gained `freeStandIn`, a look at `writtenSlots` first, and three lines of bookkeeping). What that changed for `layout --open` is in F54's Shipped note.
+- **One way of arranging.** `applyLayout` became `arrange(_:open:wait:)`, which takes one line per display, matches all their slots in one call of `WindowSlot.assign`, changes every workspace and only then lays out.
+- **Each window is moved once.** A load gives every window it docks one frame, the one it ends up with, from whichever display it comes; so does a window that takes a place that waited (`aLoadMovesEachWindowOnce`, `aWindowThatComesLateIsMovedOnceAndSoAreItsNeighbours`).
+- macOS-only, some 20 lines: `StatusMenu` marks a row that shows with ⌥ held, `ArborApp` makes the `FilePresetStore`, opens the file and fills the pasteboard. Three steps in `arbor-e2e`; `scripts/mac-shell/check-F21.sh`.
+
+Not shipped:
+- **Nothing of it has been seen on a Mac.** The macOS-only lines pass `make typecheck-mac` and have never been compiled or run there. Nobody has looked at the Presets submenu (the rows that change with ⌥), Copy Arrangement as Text, Presets File…, the name field for a preset, or `--open` with real apps. `scripts/mac-shell/check-F21.sh` has only been rehearsed on Linux (`make rehearse`); the three steps in `arbor-e2e` have not run.
+- **A preset loaded when displays come and go**: see above.
+- **Names of spaces** (F20) in a preset: the notation cannot write them yet (IDEAS I17). Names given on the display stay with the spaces that still hold the same windows after a load, like saved views, and the rest are counted in the reply.
+- A group that `rotate` has turned to run like its parent is printed (`A | (B | C)`) and read back flat, so a preset saved from it loses the group, and a name on it, when it is loaded. The frames are the same. It is the notation's to mend (`Spec.tree(filling:)`).
